Title:
Windows x64 Shellcode: TEB/PEB Internals for Runtime API Resolution

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post explains how Windows x64 shellcode can discover process and module information at runtime without relying on hardcoded WinAPI addresses (PIC requirement under ASLR). It walks through the Thread Environment Block (TEB) and how to reach the Process Environment Block (PEB) via `GS:[0x60]`, then uses `PEB->Ldr` to access `PEB_LDR_DATA` module lists. The author details the loader’s doubly-linked lists (`InMemoryOrderModuleList`, etc.) and the `LDR_DATA_TABLE_ENTRY` fields needed to retrieve a module base address (e.g., `kernel32.dll`). It compares fragile “fixed index” traversal with robust name verification using `UNICODE_STRING` length checks, case-normalization via bitmask OR, and a common ROR13 hashing approach. This is primarily useful for shellcode authors, exploit developers, and red team operators building reliable in-memory loaders. It’s important because it covers the canonical Windows user-mode primitive for module discovery that underpins most custom API-resolvers and reflective techniques.

Technical Focus:
- TEB access on x64 via GS segment (`GS:[0x60]`, `GS:[0x30]`)
- PEB structure fields (`BeingDebugged`, `Ldr`, `ImageBaseAddress`)
- `PEB_LDR_DATA` and loader module lists (circular doubly-linked lists)
- `LDR_DATA_TABLE_ENTRY` parsing (`DllBase`, `BaseDllName`, link fields)
- `UNICODE_STRING` handling (Length/Buffer) and safe string matching
- Name hashing for shellcode (ROR13) and case-insensitive normalization

Use Cases:
- Building position-independent Windows x64 shellcode that resolves modules dynamically
- Implementing a custom `kernel32.dll`/`ntdll.dll` locator prior to export parsing
- Hardening shellcode against loader order changes, EDR/AV early injection, and parallel loading
- Developing in-memory loaders/reflective components that avoid imports and static IAT usage
- Debugging and validating loader structures with WinDbg (`dt ntdll!_TEB`, `_PEB`, `_PEB_LDR_DATA`)

Keywords:
Windows x64, shellcode, position-independent code, PIC, ASLR, TEB, PEB, GS segment, NT_TIB, PEB_LDR_DATA, InMemoryOrderModuleList, LIST_ENTRY, LDR_DATA_TABLE_ENTRY, UNICODE_STRING, kernel32.dll, ntdll.dll, loader internals, ROR13 hash, WinDbg, LdrpEnableParallelLoading